            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Mandarin for weekend, then the Mandarin for Sunday."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: 年",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "年 — nián — \"year\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "一年有多少个星期 yì nián yǒu duōshao ge xīngqī — How many weeks are there in a year?"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The new character, in pieces: 年 (nián) — six strokes: a short fall, two bars, a short vertical on the left, a long bar, and one long vertical through them. The next lesson puts it in your hand."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "年 (nián) is inherited from Middle Chinese nen (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), glossed 'the grain is ripe'. One harvest a year is the usual explanation of the sense, though the step is not settled."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for times and partings."
            }
          ]
        },
